  "body": "# Summary\r\n\r\nThis ports two small optimizations from #3083 by @serialx:\r\n\r\n1. **Avoid doing the same font work multiple times.**\r\n   Previously, when a page mixed regular, bold, italic, and bold-italic text, each style could prepare characters that actually belonged to the other styles too. The new code keeps them separate, so each font style only prepares the characters it really needs.\r\n\r\n2. **Avoid temporary memory allocations while scanning a page.**\r\n   The font scanner previously created and resized temporary strings while collecting characters. It now uses a small fixed-size workspace instead. This costs about 2 KB of permanently reserved RAM, but avoids repeatedly allocating and freeing memory while pages are rendered, which should make memory usage more predictable and reduce fragmentation over time.\r\n\r\n## Results\r\n\r\nOn the mixed-style test book, normal page prewarming dropped from about **80–81 ms to 61–63 ms**, roughly a **20–25% reduction**. Total page rendering on those pages dropped from about **719 ms to 697 ms**.\r\n\r\nThe more expensive pages also improved noticeably: font preparation dropped from roughly **157–161 ms to 91–92 ms**, with total render time falling from about **950 ms to 810 ms**. The e-ink refresh itself is unchanged, so it still makes up most of the page-turn time.\r\nMemory behavior also looks much healthier during the test. Before the change, the largest available continuous block of memory fell as low as about **78 KB**. After the change it remained around **106 KB** through the same workload. \r\nThe trade-off is that the fixed workspace permanently reserves roughly **2 KB**, so this is mainly about reducing fragmentation rather than increasing headline free RAM.\r\n\r\nFor ordinary books that mostly use one font style, this should not make a noticeable difference. The benefit is mainly for pages that mix several font styles, plus more stable memory usage during longer reading sessions.\r\n\r\nMost of the LoC are the new test suite. The net code is ~170LoC\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### AI Usage\r\n\r\nWhile CrossPoint doesn't have restrictions on AI tools in contributing, please be transparent about their usage as it\r\nhelps set the right context for reviewers.\r\n\r\nDid you use AI tools to help write this code? _**< PARTIALLY >**_\r\n",
